<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babur was a Timurid prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. After losing his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> in Fergana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, he established himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. In 1526 he defeated the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> at the Battle of Panipat, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, and his memoir, the Baburnama, is a famous autobiographical work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" width="270" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: c.1650 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
